<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · deity</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pachamama</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pachamama is the earth and fertility goddess of the Andean peoples, revered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quechua">Quechua</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aymara">Aymara</a> communities since before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca Empire</a>. She is honoured with offerings, especially at planting and harvest, and her cult survived Spanish rule alongside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ecuador">Ecuador</a>'s constitution of 2008 used her name in granting legal rights to nature.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pachamama.jpg" alt="Pachamama" width="320" height="350" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Juan Santa Cruz Pachacuti · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Santa_Cruz_Pachacuti_Yamqui_Pachamama.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Pachamama, or Mama Pacha, is the Andean deity representing space-time, revered by the peoples of the Andes. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca</a> mythology and religion she is a mother goddess, linked to space, time, the universe, life, fertility and nature. The word comes from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quechua">Quechua</a>: pacha means world, universe, space-time or cosmos, and mama means mother. In various myths she is the wife of Pachacámac and the mother of Mama Quilla, the goddess of the moon, and of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inti">Inti</a>, the god of the sun. She was commonly worshipped in her earthly form as Allpamama, or goddess of the earth; her shrines consisted of sacred rocks or tree trunks, and artists showed her as a woman bearing harvests of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potato">potatoes</a> or coca leaves.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Pachamama is normally loving and generous, but she can be terrible and destructive when she is upset or hurt. She is said not to need temples or specific places of worship, since she is everywhere, though she likes springs. Fertility is a vital matter for Andean people, extending to the flowering of crops, the arrival of spring and the reproduction of animals. In exchange for her help, the shepherd of the southern Puna is obliged to offer part of what he receives, establishing a kind of reciprocity known as ayni. Allpamama is often hungry, and if she is not fed with offerings or is offended, she can cause a cosmic imbalance.</p><p>The central ritual is the Challa or Pago, meaning payment, carried out during all of August, when ritual is especially abundant before the sowing season, and in many places also on the first Friday of each month. People spill a little chicha on the ground for the goddess and drink the rest. Another day of worship is Martes de challa, which coincides with Shrove Tuesday.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>After the Spanish colonisation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>, the native populations were converted to Roman Catholicism, and through religious <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syncretism">syncretism</a> the figure of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mary-mother-of-jesus">Virgin Mary</a> was associated with Pachamama for many Indigenous peoples. Many rituals are practised together with those of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>, so that many families are at once Christian and pachamamistas. According to the scholar Manuel Marzal, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> the cult has in some cases taken on Christian characteristics, and for some Andeans Pachamama has become a symbol of the providence of one God.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-john-paul-ii">Pope John Paul II</a>, in two homilies delivered in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolivia">Bolivia</a>, on 3 February 1985 and 11 May 1988, described homage to Pachamama as an ancestral recognition of divine providence. Many Indigenous peoples in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-america">South America</a> base environmental concerns on these beliefs, saying that problems arise when people take too much from nature because they are taking too much from Pachamama.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pachamama” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The constitution of 2008 was the first in the world to grant enforceable rights to nature, or <b>Pachamama</b>, and declared Ecuador a plurinational state, ideas that have since been widely studied and imitated by environmental lawyers.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-ecuador">Republic of Ecuador · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
